# Writing curriculum content

Never answer a learning request with an enormous unstructured dump.

1. Identify the concept and prerequisites.
2. Give the simplest useful mental model.
3. Add complexity only after the simple model lands.
4. Connect related concepts (knowledge graph).
5. Implement, then break, then evaluate, then design production.

## Every major concept must eventually answer

What / why / how it works / how to implement / interactions / how it fails / secure / evaluate / scale / cost / when to use / when NOT to use / alternatives / what a production architect worries about / what a principal would challenge.

## Progressive levels (do not show all at once)

L1 Simple → L2 Logical → L3 Mechanical → L4 Implementation → L5 Engineering → L6 Architect → L7 Production → L8 Principal.

Interactive HTML (Gate 9) uses a depth selector. Markdown should still lead with L1 and disclose deeper sections.

## Four modes

**SEE** (visual) · **DO** (exercise) · **BREAK** (inject failure) · **DESIGN** (architecture challenge with no single correct answer).

## Finish every major topic with

Remember / build / experiment / what can go wrong / Senior question / Principal question / Architect challenge / learn next.

## Architect bridges

Map new AI ideas onto Kafka, Spark, APIs, state stores, data quality, tracing, and governance. An agent does not automatically replace a deterministic pipeline.

Use `.cursor/skills/contribute-concept/SKILL.md` when adding a concept. Use `99-META/templates/concept.md`. Only create template files that have real content.
